tgs:Work.ThinkingLikeAMountain a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thinking Like a Mountain: Towards a Council of All Beings"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1988 book (New Society Publishers), co-authored by John Seed, Joanna Macy, Arne Naess, and Pat Fleming — describes the Council of All Beings ritual and the philosophical/experiential foundations of deep ecology. Translated into 12+ languages. Directly discussed in RR-17 as the founding text of the Work That Reconnects lineage."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JohnSeed ;
    dct:issued "1988"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via RR-13's own real linked show notes.
#############################################

tgs:Work.FutureCouncilFilm a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Future Council"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2024 documentary film directed by Damon Gameau, following eight children (ages 11-14) across Europe by biodiesel-fueled bus, confronting corporate leaders (Nestlé, Decathlon, ING, among others) about climate accountability. Features an animated antagonist character, 'Groth,' explicitly based on Nate Hagens' own real 'Economic Superorganism' framing (tgs:Concept.Superorganism) — a direct, real instance of this graph's own foundational concept being adapted into mainstream cultural media. Premiered Melbourne International Film Festival (Aug 2024); later screened at the UN General Assembly; theatrical release planned 2025 across Australia, Europe, and America."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.DamonGameau ;
    dct:issued "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via a real Hylo community post
# (MJSullivan's own "Great Simplification" group thread), verified
# independently before minting. Real sequel to Work.HospicingModernity
# if that exists — check name below before assuming.
#############################################

tgs:Work.OutgrowingModernity a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Outgrowing Modernity: Navigating Complexity, Complicity, and Collapse with Accountability and Compassion"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2025 book by Vanessa Machado de Oliveira (Andreotti), published August 12, 2025 — the real sequel to her 2021 'Hospicing Modernity,' with contributions from Keri Facer and Awo Fatokun Faniyii. Explores 'neurocolonization' — modernity's effects on how people think, act, imagine, and adapt — and includes real documented material on the author's own collaborative work with AI systems (a recurring character, 'Aiden'). Co-founder credit: Gesturing Towards Decolonial Futures (GTDF) Arts/Research Collective."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.VanessaAndreotti ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.amazon.com/Outgrowing-Modernity-Navigating-Complexity-Accountability/dp/B0DMVDJ4VG> ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — resolves a real, long-standing PENDING item
# flagged since the very start of this project ("Scenario Thinking"
# book citation), surfaced via Frankly_139's own real show notes.
#############################################

tgs:Work.ScenarioThinkingHistoricalEvolution a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Scenario Thinking: A Historical Evolution of Strategic Foresight"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2018 academic work (Cambridge University Press, 'Elements in Business Strategy' series) by R. Bradley MacKay (University of St Andrews) and Peter McKiernan (University of Strathclyde). Traces scenario thinking's roots to ancient civilizations (including Mesopotamian and Assyrian foresight practices) through the French and Anglo-American schools of modern scenario planning, arguing scenario thinking should be understood as distinct from and more fundamental than scenario planning as a managerial technique."@en ;
    dct:creator "R. Bradley MacKay"@en, "Peter McKiernan"@en ;
    dct:issued "2018"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108571494> ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via TGS_167's own real linked show
# notes and directly discussed at length in the transcript itself.
#############################################

tgs:Work.WildEdgeOfSorrow a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Wild Edge of Sorrow: Rituals of Renewal and the Sacred Work of Grief"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Bestselling book by Francis Weller (North Atlantic Books), the real, direct source of the 'Five Gates of Grief' framework discussed at length in TGS_167. Real endorsement from Anderson Cooper ('One of the best books on grief I have ever read') and from Joanna Macy, already discussed extensively in this graph's own RR-17 build."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.FrancisWeller ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.francisweller.net/books.html> ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via TGS_213's own real linked show
# notes and directly discussed at length in the transcript itself.
#############################################

tgs:Work.TheArrogantApe a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Arrogant Ape: The Myth of Human Exceptionalism and Why It Matters"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2025 book by Christine Webb (Penguin Random House) — a real New York Times Notable Book of 2025, longlisted for the Nayef Al-Rodhan Book Prize. Argues human exceptionalism is an ideology relying more on human culture than biology, more on delusion and faith than evidence, drawing on Webb's own primatology research."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ChristineWebb ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/717436/the-arrogant-ape-by-christine-webb/> ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via TGS_138's own real linked show
# notes and directly discussed at length in the transcript itself.
#############################################

tgs:Work.TaoOfPhysics a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Tao of Physics: An Exploration of the Parallels Between Modern Physics and Eastern Mysticism"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1975 book by Fritjof Capra, his real first and best-known work — still in print in 40+ editions, referenced alongside a statue of Shiva in the courtyard of CERN. Argues for real parallels between quantum theory/relativity and Eastern mystical traditions (Hinduism, Buddhism, Taoism), directly inspired by his own real undergraduate reading of Werner Heisenberg's 'Physics and Philosophy.'"@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.FritjofCapra ;
    dct:issued "1975"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.fritjofcapra.net/about/> ;
    .

# added 2026-08-25

tgs:Work.MaterialWorld a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Material World: The Six Raw Materials That Shape Modern Civilization"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2023 book by Ed Conway (Penguin Random House / WH Allen). An Economist and Sunday Times Book of the Year, shortlisted for the 2023 FT Business Book of the Year Award. Traces the full extraction-to-product supply chain of six materials — sand, salt, iron, copper, oil, and lithium — arguing these physically underpin the modern economy far out of proportion to their small direct share of GDP."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.EdConway ;
    dct:issued "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Material_World_(book)> ;
    .

# added 2026-08-29

tgs:Work.DeseretFarmerBailout2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "There's no bailout for the American Farmer"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Deseret Magazine, March 2026. Documents structural farm consolidation via 'get big or get out' market logic — 63 farms lost per day, 162,000 farms lost 2017-2024, 24 million acres lost. Reports that even government bailouts have failed to reverse the trend. Real, direct thematic overlap with TGS_198's own discussion of concentrated economic power squeezing out smaller, locally-owned businesses."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.deseret.com/magazine/2026/03/07/america-family-farms-in-decline/> ;
    dct:issued "2026-03"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.WorldBankFoodSecurityUpdate2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Food Security Update"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "World Bank, June 2026. Authoritative, non-partisan monitoring brief documenting real global fertilizer-price pressure (35% increase) and food-supply fragility. Descriptive rather than prescriptive — doesn't itself argue for local food systems as the remedy. Real, direct connection to TGS_198's own discussion of supply-chain fragility (the single-slaughterhouse and baby-formula-plant examples)."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/foodsecurity> ;
    dct:issued "2026-06"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.PewDecliningTrust2025 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Americans' Declining Trust in Each Other and Reasons Behind It"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Pew Research Center, May 2025. Documents declining interpersonal trust connected to political polarization, including spatial/social sorting that reduces meaningful cross-party contact. Descriptive survey data, not prescriptive. TGS_198 supplies a real causal mechanism this piece's data doesn't on its own — Stacy Mitchell's own direct argument connecting economic powerlessness to democratic erosion."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2025/05/08/americans-declining-trust-in-each-other-and-reasons-behind-it/> ;
    dct:issued "2025-05"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.CarbonMajorsWaterCrisis2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Carbon emissions exacerbating the Western US water crisis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Williams, E.L., Abatzoglou, J.T., Phillips, C.A., Safeeq, M., Sadai, S., Chegwidden, O.S., Fernandez-Bou, A.S., Merner, L.D., Ortiz-Partida, J.P. Communications Earth & Environment 7, 675 (2026), published by Nature Portfolio, Union of Concerned Scientists and UC Merced. First attribution study of its kind: finds 40-64% of climate-driven western US water crisis impacts (1950-2024) — snowpack loss, streamflow decline, irrigation demand increase — attributable to 122 'Carbon Majors' (fossil fuel and cement companies), including roughly one-third of California Central Valley's climate-related groundwater decline. Open Access, CC-BY 4.0 — direct quotation with attribution explicitly permitted. Source article for WWNS #1 ('The 122 Companies Behind the Western Water Crisis')."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.nature.com/articles/s43247-026-03900-6> ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode> ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:WWNSArticle.WWNS_1 ;
    .

tgs:Work.Ultrasocial2021 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ultrasocial: The Evolution of Human Nature and the Quest for a Sustainable Future"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "John Gowdy, Cambridge University Press, October 2021. Argues human 'ultrasociality' — the same evolutionary category as ants, termites, and other eusocial species — emerged from the economic surplus dynamics of agriculture, not from a fixed 'selfish gene' human nature. Co-authored research on ants/social insects with Lisi Krall (SUNY Cortland, not yet built in this graph) underlies much of the book's argument. Directly cited in TGS_14's own real show notes."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JohnGowdy ;
    dct:source <https://www.amazon.com/Ultrasocial-Evolution-Nature-Sustainable-Future/dp/110883826X> ;
    dct:issued "2021-10"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.PsychopathicSelectionHypothesisBook a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Greg Elliott's own book, self-described in the TGS_231 transcript as published 'back in May' (year not independently confirmed — the episode's own real, wider context suggests 2026, consistent with dct:issued below, but not verified via direct fetch). Argues dark triad traits (psychopathy, Machiavellianism, narcissism) are selected for at an institutional/civilizational scale via game-theoretic dynamics, accelerating with the agricultural revolution and again with fossil fuel expansion. Endorsed by William Rees (already a real Persona in this graph, RR-2) — the two are described in the transcript as having 'become pretty close friends' as a result."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.GregElliott ;
    .
